# Modules Structured Reference

This file reorganizes module knowledge into the same keyed mechanic format used by other deterministic knowledge sources.
The original raw source remains preserved in public/knowledge/modules for side-by-side comparison.

Mechanic: Modules
Type: System
Domain: Progression
Section: Modules
Category: Gear
Aliases: none.
Definition: Modules are equipable gear items that provide main effects, sub-module effects, and sometimes unique effects.
Behavior:
Modules come in four types: Cannon, Armor, Generator, and Core.
Modules are unlocked at Tier 2 Wave 90.
Modules can be acquired through module tickets or Gems.

Mechanic: Module Types
Type: Classification
Domain: Modules
Section: Modules
Category: Families
Aliases: module families
Definition: Module Types split modules into Cannon, Armor, Generator, and Core families.
Behavior:
Cannon modules focus on Attack.
Armor modules focus on Defense.
Generator modules focus on Utility.
Core modules focus on Ultimate Weapons.

Mechanic: Module Acquisition
Type: Acquisition System
Domain: Modules
Section: Modules
Category: Pulls
Aliases: acquiring modules
Definition: Module Acquisition is the process of unlocking and drawing modules with tickets or Gems.
Behavior:
Modules unlock at Tier 2 Wave 90.
Players receive 10 module tickets when modules first unlock.
Modules can be bought for 20 Gems as a single draw or 200 Gems as a ten-pull.

Mechanic: Module Pull Rates
Type: Draw Rates
Domain: Modules
Section: Modules
Category: Pulls
Aliases: module rates
Definition: Module Pull Rates determine the rarity distribution for purchased module draws.
Behavior:
Common module rate is 68.5%.
Rare module rate is 29%.
Epic module rate is 2.5%.
Bad luck protection guarantees an Epic module if 150 purchases pass without one.
Every ten-pull guarantees at least one Rare module.

Mechanic: Module Banners
Type: Banner System
Domain: Modules
Section: Modules
Category: Pulls
Aliases: featured banner; standard banner
Definition: Module Banners control which module pools and rate modifiers are active for module pulls.
Behavior:
The standard banner uses normal Epic pull distributions.
Modules already owned at Ancestral 5 stars are less likely to be pulled on the standard banner.
The featured banner gives 50% of Epic pulls to the featured module.
Featured banners last two weeks and are followed by a one-week break.

Mechanic: Module Upgrading
Type: Upgrade System
Domain: Modules
Section: Modules
Category: Progression
Aliases: leveling modules
Definition: Module Upgrading uses shards and coins to raise a module's level and improve its main stat.
Behavior:
Module leveling increases the main stat only.
The maximum level depends on the module's current rarity.
Merging to a higher rarity raises the level cap without resetting the current level.

Mechanic: Module Level Caps
Type: Level Cap Table
Domain: Modules
Section: Modules
Category: Progression
Aliases: max module level
Definition: Module Level Caps define the highest module level available at each rarity tier.
Behavior:
Rare caps at level 30.
Rare+ caps at level 40.
Epic caps at level 60.
Epic+ caps at level 80.
Legendary caps at level 100.
Legendary+ caps at level 120.
Mythic caps at level 140.
Mythic+ caps at level 160.
Ancestral caps at level 200.
Ancestral star tiers continue to 300 at Ancestral 5.

Mechanic: Module Restore
Type: Reset Rule
Domain: Modules
Section: Modules
Category: Progression
Aliases: restore button; reset modules
Definition: Module Restore resets level investment and returns shard and coin spending.
Behavior:
Module Restore costs no resources.
Module Restore returns all spent shards and coins.
Reroll shards and Gems are not returned.
Sub-module effects are retained until the module is leveled again, even if those effects normally require higher levels.

Mechanic: Module Boss Drops
Type: Drop System
Domain: Bosses
Section: Modules
Category: Drops
Aliases: boss module drops
Definition: Module Boss Drops determine boss rewards for reroll shards and module drops.
Behavior:
Bosses can drop reroll shards as well as Common and Rare modules.
Reroll shards have a 15% drop chance.
Common modules start at a 2% drop chance.
Rare modules start at a 0.5% drop chance.
Boss reroll shard quantity increases by tier.
Module drop rates can be increased by labs.

Mechanic: Module Merging
Type: Merge System
Domain: Modules
Section: Modules
Category: Progression
Aliases: merging modules
Definition: Module Merging raises a module's rarity, main stat value, scaling, and level cap.
Behavior:
Common modules cannot be merged.
Rare modules can merge up to Legendary+.
Epic modules can merge up to Ancestral 5 stars.
Merging does not reset the current module level.
Rare modules never gain unique effects through merging.
Rare modules keep their original name and do not become matching Epic uniques.

Mechanic: Module Merge Materials
Type: Merge Materials
Domain: Modules
Section: Modules
Category: Progression
Aliases: merge materials; wildcard modules
Definition: Module Merge Materials specify the exact same-module and wildcard requirements for each merge step.
Behavior:
The primary module is always the first module selected for merging.
A module icon requirement means the material must be that same named module at the shown rarity.
A wildcard requirement can be any module of the same type and shown rarity.
Ancestral star upgrades require a natural Epic+ copy of the same module and each star increases the main effect.

Mechanic: Module Stats
Type: Stat Structure
Domain: Modules
Section: Modules
Category: Effects
Aliases: module stat types
Definition: Module Stats are split into Main Effect, Sub-Module Effects, and Unique Effects.
Behavior:
Every module has a Main Effect.
Modules can unlock Sub-Module Effect slots as they level.
Unique Effects only exist on naturally drawn Epic modules.

Mechanic: Module Main Effect
Type: Main Stat
Domain: Modules
Section: Modules
Category: Effects
Aliases: main effect
Definition: Module Main Effect is the primary stat bonus granted by a module and the only effect increased by module level.
Behavior:
Main Effect maps to tower damage for Cannon, health for Armor, coin bonus for Generator, and Ultimate Weapon damage for Core.
Only module level upgrades change the Main Effect.
Module levels 41, 101, 141, 161, 201, and 241 unlock additional Sub-Module Effect slots.

Mechanic: Module Sub-Module Effects
Type: Substats
Domain: Modules
Section: Modules
Category: Effects
Aliases: substats; sub-module effects
Definition: Module Sub-Module Effects are rerollable secondary stats chosen from a family-specific pool.
Behavior:
Sub-Module Effect pools depend on the module family.
The maximum rarity of a substat matches the module's current rarity, but the current rolled substat can be below that ceiling.
Substat rarity chances are Common 46.2%, Rare 40%, Epic 10%, Legendary 2.5%, Mythic 1.0%, and Ancestral 0.3%.

Mechanic: Module Reroll Locks
Type: Reroll Cost Rule
Domain: Modules
Section: Modules
Category: Effects
Aliases: lock costs; locked substats
Definition: Module Reroll Locks protect chosen substats from rerolling at the cost of higher reroll-shard prices.
Behavior:
Zero locks costs 10 reroll shards.
One lock costs 40 reroll shards.
Two locks costs 160 reroll shards.
Three locks costs 500 reroll shards.
Four locks costs 1000 reroll shards.
Five locks costs 1600 reroll shards.
Six locks costs 2250 reroll shards.
Seven locks costs 3000 reroll shards.
Auto Roll can reroll rapidly until acceptable effects and rarities are found.
Effect bans remove banned effects from the reroll pool when researched.

Mechanic: Unique Effects
Type: Unique Effect Rule
Domain: Modules
Section: Modules
Category: Effects
Aliases: unique module effects
Definition: Unique Effects are special module abilities present only on modules that were naturally drawn as Epics.
Behavior:
Epic modules drawn from pulls have Unique Effects.
Rare modules merged to Epic or higher never gain Unique Effects.
Natural Epic modules retain their Unique Effects through higher rarities.

Mechanic: Assist Modules
Type: Assist System
Domain: Modules
Section: Modules
Category: Assist Modules
Aliases: assist module; assist slot
Definition: Assist Modules let a second module be equipped in the same slot for weaker versions of its effects.
Behavior:
Assist Modules unlock at Tier 19 Wave 40.
Assist slots cost 1000 stones each to unlock.
An assist module cannot share the same name as the main module in that slot.
Assist module level is separate from the main module level.
Assist efficiency can be improved with stones and with assist-module labs unlocked at Tier 19 Wave 50.
Assist modules do not bypass hard caps such as defense, Chrono Field slow, Wall rebuild, Shockwave Frequency, Death Defy, or Inner Land Mine cooldown caps.
As of v27.1, all assist-module multiplier stats are multiplicative.
Quantity assist substats use FLOOR, not ROUND, so values only add quantity once the floored threshold is reached.

Mechanic: Assist Module Unique Rarity Costs
Type: Stone Cost Ladder
Domain: Stones
Section: Modules
Category: Assist Modules
Aliases: assist rarity cost
Definition: Assist Module Unique Rarity Costs define the stone spending required to raise assist unique-effect quality.
Behavior:
Unlocking assist unique effect quality at Epic costs 1000 stones.
Raising it to Legendary costs 1000 stones.
Raising it to Mythic costs 1200 stones.
Raising it to Ancestral costs 1400 stones.
The listed total is 4600 stones.
